## Why This Is Asked

This question probes end-to-end design of a multi-tenant LLM-ops pipeline balancing latency, privacy, policy management, and observability. It assesses ability to design streaming prompts, per-tenant models, hot-updatable policies, drift alerts, and tests.

## Key Concepts

- Per-tenant policy engine
- Data locality and privacy controls
- Streaming prompt handling
- Dynamic model routing
- Drift detection and audits
- Tamper-evident logs and auditable trails

## Code Example

```javascript
// Example: pick a model based on policy
function selectModel(tenant, policy) {
  const p = policy[tenant] || {};
  if (p.forceGuard) return 'safety-guarded';
  if (p.latencyBudget && p.latencyBudget < 75) return 'base';
  return p.preferredModel ?? 'base';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions

- How would you validate drift triggers in production without constant retraining?
- What would be your approach to simulate hot policy updates during peak load?

## Why This Is Asked
Tests real-world hybrid edge-cloud serving with data locality, SLA management, and per-tenant isolation under fluctuating network conditions.

## Key Concepts
- Data locality and residency per tenant
- Edge vs cloud routing and burst handling
- Per-tenant model pools and versioning
- Observability, fault tolerance, and rollback strategies

## Code Example
```javascript
// Pseudo policy evaluator
function routeRequest(req, policyStore){
  const p = policyStore.get(req.tenantId);
  if(p.locality === 'edge' && req.latencyBudget >= EDGE_LATENCY){
    return 'edge';
  }
  return 'cloud';
}
```

## Follow-up Questions
- How would you validate locality guarantees under variable networks?
- How would you automate canary rollouts and safe rollback per tenant?

